IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- South Carolina Income Tax Act (S.C. Code Ann. tit. 12, ch. 6) -- the individual income tax as it falls on a resident
-- individual: who must file (s 12-6-4910(1)), by when (s 12-6-4970(A), s 12-6-4980, s 12-6-4990), joint or separate
-- (s 12-6-5000), the starting point in federal taxable income (s 12-6-40, s 12-6-1110), the main modifications
-- (ss 12-6-1120, 12-6-1130(2)), the individual deductions (ss 12-6-1140(13), 12-6-1150, 12-6-1160, 12-6-1170), the rates
-- (s 12-6-510(B), with the Legislative Council's 2025 Tax Computation Schedule printed under it), and three credits
-- (s 12-6-3330 two wage earner, s 12-6-3380 child and dependent care, s 12-6-3632 earned income tax credit).
--
-- SEAT: the individual taxpayer (a resident of South Carolina, alone or with a spouse on a joint return). "Must I file?
-- By when? What is my South Carolina taxable income, and how much tax do I owe?" The Department of Revenue's duties are
-- encoded for what they mean to the taxpayer.
--
-- Edition: S.C. Code of Laws Unannotated, Title 12, Chapter 6, "current through the 2025 Session", as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/sc-code-title-12-chapter-6-income-tax-act.txt (retrieved 2026-10-19).
--
-- Federal figures (federal taxable income, the federal EITC, the IRC s 63 standard deduction, the s 151(d) exemption
-- amount, the IRC s 21 expenses) are borrowed terms asked as facts. The federal Internal Revenue Code encoding is at
-- subjects/us/internal-revenue-code-of-1986/; cross-folder IMPORT is not possible in the stable l4.
--
-- NOT ENCODED (answer `not covered` where a question can reach them): nonresidents and part-year residents
-- (ss 12-6-1710, 12-6-1720); rate schedules for any tax year other than 2025 unless the person supplies the department's
-- published figures; the other credits of Article 25; s 12-6-1171 (military retirement) and the surviving-spouse limb of
-- s 12-6-1170; estates, trusts, corporations; withholding (ch. 12-8); penalties (ch. 12-54).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the individual's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `South Carolina Income Tax Act -- the individual income tax`

§§ `Answers and what is not covered`

DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `a nonresident or part-year resident computes South Carolina taxable income under Article 13 (ss 12-6-1710, 12-6-1720), which is not encoded`
    `the rate schedule for that tax year is not in the deposited text: give the department's published figures for the year`
    `the rates for tax years 2022 to 2024 depend on the top-rate phase-down in s 12-6-510(B)(3), which is not encoded`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

DECLARE `Residence` IS ONE OF
    -- s 12-6-30(2): "Resident individual" means an individual domiciled in this State.
    `resident for the whole year`
    -- s 12-6-30(3): a resident individual for only a portion of the tax year.
    `part-year resident`
    -- s 12-6-30(2): an individual other than a resident individual or a part-year resident.
    `nonresident`

-- The federal filing status (IRC s 1, s 2, s 7703, borrowed). s 12-6-5000: the South Carolina return follows the federal one.
DECLARE `Filing status` IS ONE OF
    single
    `married filing jointly`
    `married filing separately`
    `head of household`
    `surviving spouse`

§§ `s 12-6-4910(1) -- must I file a South Carolina return`

-- s 12-6-4910(1)(a): an individual not in (c) "who has a gross income for the taxable year of at least the federal exemption
-- amount plus the applicable basic standard deduction, plus any deduction the taxpayer qualifies for pursuant to Section
-- 12-6-1170(B), without regard to a reduction for the retirement income deduction", and whose status is single, surviving
-- spouse or head of household, or married filing separately with a spouse who does not itemize.
-- (b): on a joint return, "combined gross income ... more than the sum of twice the exemption amount plus the applicable
-- basic standard deduction ... plus any deduction the taxpayer qualifies for pursuant to Section 12-6-1170(B)"; the standard
-- deduction is increased for 65 or older under IRC s 63(c)(3), (f)(1) (asked as part of the basic standard deduction).
-- (a) says "at least", (b) says "more than" (NOTES.md finding 3).
-- (c): a dependent with unearned income over the IRC s 63(c)(5)(A) amount or gross income over the standard deduction, a
-- short-period return under IRC s 443(a)(1), or an individual whose standard deduction is zero: files if gross income
-- "exceeds the federal personal exemption amount".
-- (d): "a nonresident individual with South Carolina gross income greater than the personal exemption amount".
-- (e)(ii): the exemption amount is IRC s 151(d)'s; zero for an individual described in s 151(d)(2) (a dependent).
GIVEN `filing status` IS A `Filing status`
      `I am in s 12-6-4910(1)(c)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my spouse itemizes` IS A BOOLEAN
      `gross income` IS A NUMBER
      `federal exemption amount` IS A NUMBER
      `basic standard deduction` IS A NUMBER
      `s 12-6-1170(B) deduction I qualify for` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a resident individual must file` IF
    IF `I am in s 12-6-4910(1)(c)`
    THEN `gross income` GREATER THAN `federal exemption amount`
    ELSE CONSIDER `filing status`
         WHEN `married filing jointly` THEN
             `gross income` GREATER THAN ((2 TIMES `federal exemption amount`) PLUS `basic standard deduction` PLUS `s 12-6-1170(B) deduction I qualify for`)
         WHEN `married filing separately` THEN
             IF `my spouse itemizes`
             -- my standard deduction is then zero (IRC s 63(c)(6)(A)), which puts me in (c)(iii)
             THEN `gross income` GREATER THAN `federal exemption amount`
             ELSE `gross income` AT LEAST (`federal exemption amount` PLUS `basic standard deduction` PLUS `s 12-6-1170(B) deduction I qualify for`)
         OTHERWISE `gross income` AT LEAST (`federal exemption amount` PLUS `basic standard deduction` PLUS `s 12-6-1170(B) deduction I qualify for`)

@export Must I file a South Carolina individual income tax return for the year (S.C. Code s 12-6-4910(1))?
GIVEN `my residence` IS A `Residence` @desc Were you domiciled in South Carolina for the whole year ("resident for the whole year"), for part of it ("part-year resident"), or not at all ("nonresident")?
      `my filing status` IS A `Filing status` @desc Your federal filing status for the year: single, married filing jointly, married filing separately, head of household, or surviving spouse
      `my gross income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your gross income for the year in US dollars, as defined for federal tax (combined with your spouse's if you file jointly); if you are a nonresident, your South Carolina gross income
      `I am in s 12-6-4910(1)(c)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of these is true: someone else can claim you as a dependent and your unearned income is over the IRC s 63(c)(5)(A) amount or your gross income is over your standard deduction; your federal standard deduction is zero for a reason other than your spouse itemizing; or you are filing for less than 12 months because you changed your accounting period
      `my spouse itemizes` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are married filing separately, whether your spouse itemizes deductions (ignored otherwise)
      `the federal exemption amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The federal personal exemption amount under IRC s 151(d) for the year in US dollars (zero for tax years 2018 to 2025), or zero if you are someone's dependent
      `my basic standard deduction` IS A NUMBER @desc Your federal basic standard deduction under IRC s 63(c) for your filing status, including the additional amount for being 65 or older or blind, in US dollars
      `my age 65 deduction under s 12-6-1170(B)` IS A NUMBER @desc If you (or your spouse, on a joint return) reach 65 this year, the deduction you qualify for under s 12-6-1170(B) before any reduction for the retirement income deduction: $15,000 for each spouse aged 65 or more; otherwise 0
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I file a South Carolina return` IF
    CONSIDER `my residence`
    WHEN `nonresident` THEN `my gross income` GREATER THAN `the federal exemption amount`
    OTHERWISE `a resident individual must file` `my filing status` `I am in s 12-6-4910(1)(c)` `my spouse itemizes` `my gross income` `the federal exemption amount` `my basic standard deduction` `my age 65 deduction under s 12-6-1170(B)`

§§ `ss 12-6-4970, 12-6-4980, 12-6-4990 -- by when I must file and pay`

-- s 12-6-4970(A): returns "must be filed on or before the fifteenth day of the fourth month following the taxable year".
-- (E): an electronic return has the same due date.
GIVEN `the last day of my taxable year` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the return due date` MEANS
    (`add months` (`the last day of my taxable year` PLUS 1) 3) PLUS 14

@export By what date must I file my South Carolina individual return (S.C. Code s 12-6-4970(A))?
GIVEN `the last day of my tax year` IS A DATE @desc The last day of your taxable year, usually 31 December of the tax year
GIVETH A DATE
`When is my South Carolina return due` MEANS `the return due date` `the last day of my tax year`

-- s 12-6-4980(A): "The department may allow an extension of time not to exceed six months for filing returns". (B): a taxpayer
-- with a federal extension who owes no payment need not apply. "Taxes shown to be due on a return ... must be paid at the
-- time the return is due to be filed, without regard to an extension". s 12-6-4990: "the taxpayer shall pay the tax due with
-- the return ... at the time for filing the return determined without regard to any extensions".
GIVEN `the last day of my taxable year` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the extended due date` MEANS
    `add months` (`the return due date` `the last day of my taxable year`) 6

DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `the taxpayer`
    `the Department of Revenue`

DECLARE `Act` IS ONE OF
    `file the South Carolina return`
    `pay the tax due`

-- The duty to file. Time counts in days from the last day of the taxable year. With an extension allowed by the department
-- (or a federal extension where no payment is due, s 12-6-4980(B)), the deadline is the extended due date instead.
-- (Written as one deadline, not as a second obligation under LEST, because the stable l4 would start a LEST obligation's
-- clock only when it reads a later event.) Penalties are in chapter 54 of Title 12, not deposited: the breach is named only.
GIVEN `the last day of my taxable year` IS A DATE
      `I have an extension` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act`
`my duty to file` MEANS
    PARTY `the taxpayer`
    MUST  `file the South Carolina return`
    WITHIN (IF `I have an extension`
            THEN (`the extended due date` `the last day of my taxable year`) MINUS `the last day of my taxable year`
            ELSE (`the return due date` `the last day of my taxable year`) MINUS `the last day of my taxable year`)
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the taxpayer` BECAUSE (IF `I have an extension`
                                             THEN "the return was not filed by the extended due date (s 12-6-4980(A))"
                                             ELSE "the return was not filed on or before the fifteenth day of the fourth month after the taxable year (s 12-6-4970(A))")

-- The duty to pay, which an extension does not move (s 12-6-4980(B), s 12-6-4990).
GIVEN `the last day of my taxable year` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act`
`my duty to pay` MEANS
    PARTY `the taxpayer`
    MUST  `pay the tax due`
    WITHIN (`the return due date` `the last day of my taxable year`) MINUS `the last day of my taxable year`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the taxpayer` BECAUSE "the tax due was not paid by the original due date; an extension does not extend the time to pay (ss 12-6-4980(B), 12-6-4990)"

@export If I have an extension of time to file, by what date must I file my South Carolina return (S.C. Code s 12-6-4980)?
GIVEN `the last day of my tax year` IS A DATE @desc The last day of your taxable year, usually 31 December of the tax year
GIVETH A DATE
`When is my extended return due` MEANS `the extended due date` `the last day of my tax year`

@export If I have an extension of time to file, by what date must I still pay my South Carolina tax (S.C. Code ss 12-6-4980(B), 12-6-4990)?
GIVEN `the last day of my tax year` IS A DATE @desc The last day of your taxable year, usually 31 December of the tax year
GIVETH A DATE
`When must I pay even with an extension` MEANS `the return due date` `the last day of my tax year`

§§ `s 12-6-5000 -- joint or separate return`

DECLARE `Which South Carolina return` IS ONE OF
    `a joint South Carolina return, because our federal taxable income is determined on a joint federal return (s 12-6-5000(B), (C)(1))`
    `separate South Carolina returns, because our federal taxable income is determined on separate federal returns (s 12-6-5000(A))`
    `a separate South Carolina return, because the nonresident spouse's state does not allow a South Carolina resident a joint return (s 12-6-5000(C)(2))`
    `separate returns, unless we both elect a joint South Carolina return (s 12-6-5000(D))`

@export As a married couple, do we file a joint or separate South Carolina return (S.C. Code s 12-6-5000)?
GIVEN `we file a federal return` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and your spouse file a federal income tax return for the year (jointly or separately)
      `our federal return is joint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your federal taxable income is determined on a joint federal return
      `one of us is a nonresident whose state does not allow a joint return with a South Carolina resident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a spouse who is not a South Carolina resident lives in a state that does not allow its residents to file a joint return with a South Carolina resident spouse
GIVETH A `Which South Carolina return`
`Joint or separate South Carolina return` MEANS
    IF (NOT `we file a federal return`)
    THEN `separate returns, unless we both elect a joint South Carolina return (s 12-6-5000(D))`
    ELSE IF (NOT `our federal return is joint`)
         THEN `separate South Carolina returns, because our federal taxable income is determined on separate federal returns (s 12-6-5000(A))`
         ELSE IF `one of us is a nonresident whose state does not allow a joint return with a South Carolina resident`
              THEN `a separate South Carolina return, because the nonresident spouse's state does not allow a South Carolina resident a joint return (s 12-6-5000(C)(2))`
              ELSE `a joint South Carolina return, because our federal taxable income is determined on a joint federal return (s 12-6-5000(B), (C)(1))`

§§ `ss 12-6-1170 -- the retirement income and age 65 deductions, for one individual`

-- s 12-6-1170(A)(1): "An individual taxpayer who is the original owner of a qualified retirement account is allowed an annual
-- deduction ... of not more than three thousand dollars of retirement income received. Beginning in the year in which the
-- taxpayer reaches age sixty-five, the taxpayer may deduct not more than ten thousand dollars". (A)(2): retirement income
-- is otherwise taxable income, not subject to an early-distribution penalty, from IRC ss 401, 403, 408, 457 plans and public
-- employee plans including military retirement.
GIVEN `I reach 65 this year` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my retirement income` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`my retirement income deduction` MEANS
    min `my retirement income` (IF `I reach 65 this year` THEN 10000 ELSE 3000)

-- s 12-6-1170(B): "Beginning for the taxable year during which a resident individual taxpayer attains the age of sixty-five
-- years, the resident individual taxpayer is allowed a deduction ... not to exceed fifteen thousand dollars reduced by any
-- amount the taxpayer deducts pursuant to subsection (A)". On a joint return the maximum is $15,000 where one spouse is 65
-- and $30,000 where both are; this encoding applies the (A) reduction spouse by spouse (NOTES.md finding 5). The deduction
-- is "from South Carolina taxable income received", so it cannot exceed the income: the overall taxable income is floored
-- at zero below.
GIVEN `I reach 65 this year` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my retirement income` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`my age 65 deduction` MEANS
    IF `I reach 65 this year`
    THEN max 0 (15000 MINUS (`my retirement income deduction` `I reach 65 this year` `my retirement income`))
    ELSE 0

§§ `ss 12-6-1110 to 12-6-1160 -- South Carolina taxable income`

-- s 12-6-1110: gross income, adjusted gross income and taxable income "as calculated under the Internal Revenue Code are
-- modified as provided in this article". The starting point is federal taxable income (s 12-6-40(A)(1)(a): the IRC as
-- amended through 31 December 2024).
DECLARE `My year` HAS
    `federal taxable income`                                     IS A NUMBER
    -- s 12-6-1120(1): only interest on this State's and its subdivisions' obligations, and on US obligations, is exempt.
    `interest on other states' obligations excluded federally`   IS A NUMBER
    `interest on US obligations included federally`              IS A NUMBER
    -- s 12-6-1120(2)
    `state income tax refund included federally`                 IS A NUMBER
    -- s 12-6-1120(4): no IRC s 86
    `social security and tier 1 railroad benefits taxed federally` IS A NUMBER
    -- s 12-6-1130(2)
    `I itemized federally`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `state and local income taxes in my federal itemized deductions` IS A NUMBER
    `my federal itemized deductions`                             IS A NUMBER
    `the standard deduction I would have had`                    IS A NUMBER
    -- s 12-6-1150
    `net capital gain`                                           IS A NUMBER
    -- s 12-6-1140(13), s 12-6-1160
    `dependents`                                                 IS A NUMBER
    `dependents under six`                                       IS A NUMBER
    `the dependent exemption amount for the year`                IS A NUMBER
    -- s 12-6-1170, for me and (on a joint return) my spouse
    `I reach 65 this year`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `my retirement income`                                       IS A NUMBER
    `my spouse is on this return`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `my spouse reaches 65 this year`                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `my spouse's retirement income`                              IS A NUMBER

-- s 12-6-1130(2): no deduction for state and local income taxes; "This modification is limited for individual taxpayers to
-- the excess of itemized deductions over the standard deduction that would be allowed".
GIVEN y IS A `My year`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 12-6-1130(2) addition` MEANS
    IF y's `I itemized federally`
    THEN max 0 (min (y's `state and local income taxes in my federal itemized deductions`)
                    (y's `my federal itemized deductions` MINUS y's `the standard deduction I would have had`))
    ELSE 0

GIVEN y IS A `My year`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the additions` MEANS
    y's `interest on other states' obligations excluded federally` PLUS `the s 12-6-1130(2) addition` y

-- s 12-6-1150(A): "a deduction ... equal to forty-four percent of net capital gain".
-- s 12-6-1140(13): "a South Carolina dependent exemption equal to four thousand one hundred ten dollars for each eligible
-- dependent", adjusted each December 15 by chained CPI (the adjusted figure is an input). s 12-6-1160: an additional
-- deduction of the same amount for each such dependent who "has not yet attained the age of six years".
GIVEN y IS A `My year`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the subtractions` MEANS
        y's `interest on US obligations included federally`
    PLUS y's `state income tax refund included federally`
    PLUS y's `social security and tier 1 railroad benefits taxed federally`
    PLUS (44% TIMES (max 0 (y's `net capital gain`)))
    PLUS (y's `dependents` TIMES y's `the dependent exemption amount for the year`)
    PLUS (y's `dependents under six` TIMES y's `the dependent exemption amount for the year`)
    PLUS `my retirement income deduction` (y's `I reach 65 this year`) (y's `my retirement income`)
    PLUS `my age 65 deduction` (y's `I reach 65 this year`) (y's `my retirement income`)
    PLUS (IF y's `my spouse is on this return`
          THEN     `my retirement income deduction` (y's `my spouse reaches 65 this year`) (y's `my spouse's retirement income`)
              PLUS `my age 65 deduction` (y's `my spouse reaches 65 this year`) (y's `my spouse's retirement income`)
          ELSE 0)

GIVEN y IS A `My year`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`South Carolina taxable income` MEANS
    max 0 ((y's `federal taxable income` PLUS `the additions` y) MINUS `the subtractions` y)

§§ `s 12-6-510 -- the rates`

-- s 12-6-510(B)(1), "for taxable years beginning after 2021", with brackets indexed under s 12-6-520:
--   at least $0 but less than $3,200: 0%; $3,200 to $16,040: "3% times the amount minus $96"; $16,040 or more: "6% times
--   the amount minus $577". (B)(3): the top rate was 6.5% for 2022 and falls by 0.1 point a year on a revenue trigger
--   until it is 6%.
-- The Legislative Council's Editor's Note under s 12-6-510 prints the "2025 TAX COMPUTATION SCHEDULE": at least $0 but less
-- than $3,560: 0%; $3,560 to $17,830: "3% times the amount minus $107"; $17,830 or more: "6.0% times the amount minus $642".
-- So for 2025 the top rate is fully phased in at 6% (NOTES.md finding 1).
DECLARE `Rate schedule` HAS
    `the 3 percent bracket starts at`        IS A NUMBER
    `the top bracket starts at`              IS A NUMBER
    `subtracted in the 3 percent bracket`    IS A NUMBER
    `the top rate`                           IS A NUMBER
    `subtracted in the top bracket`          IS A NUMBER

`the 2025 Tax Computation Schedule` MEANS `Rate schedule` WITH
    `the 3 percent bracket starts at`        IS 3560
    `the top bracket starts at`              IS 17830
    `subtracted in the 3 percent bracket`    IS 107
    `the top rate`                           IS 6%
    `subtracted in the top bracket`          IS 642

GIVEN s IS A `Rate schedule`
      `taxable income` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`tax by the schedule` MEANS
    IF `taxable income` LESS THAN s's `the 3 percent bracket starts at`
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `taxable income` LESS THAN s's `the top bracket starts at`
         -- floored at zero: at the very bottom of the 3% bracket the printed formula gives a few cents below zero
         -- (3% of $3,560 is $106.80, less $107), which no rule makes payable or refundable (NOTES.md finding 2)
         THEN max 0 ((3% TIMES `taxable income`) MINUS s's `subtracted in the 3 percent bracket`)
         ELSE (s's `the top rate` TIMES `taxable income`) MINUS s's `subtracted in the top bracket`

§§ `ss 12-6-3330, 12-6-3380, 12-6-3632 -- credits`

-- s 12-6-3632: "a nonrefundable credit against the tax imposed pursuant to Section 12-6-510 on a full-year resident
-- individual taxpayer an amount equal to one hundred twenty-five percent of the federal earned income tax credit (EITC)
-- allowed the taxpayer pursuant to Internal Revenue Code Section 32." (Phased in to 2023 by 2017 Act No. 40 s 16.B.)
-- s 12-6-3330(A), (B): married individuals filing jointly, both with South Carolina earned income: "seven-tenths of one
-- percent multiplied by the lesser of: (1) fifty thousand dollars; or (2) the South Carolina qualified earned income of the
-- spouse with the lower South Carolina qualified earned income".
-- s 12-6-3380: the IRC s 21 credit "except that the term 'applicable percentage' means seven percent and is not reduced";
-- the IRC s 21 creditable expenses (after its dollar limits) are an input.
-- Only s 12-6-3632 says "nonrefundable"; ss 12-6-3330 and 12-6-3380 say nothing either way. Each is a credit "against" the
-- tax, and none is said to be refundable, so all three are applied only up to the tax (NOTES.md finding 6).
GIVEN `filing status` IS A `Filing status`
      `my qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER
      `my spouse's qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the two wage earner credit` MEANS
    IF     `filing status` EQUALS `married filing jointly`
       AND `my qualified earned income` GREATER THAN 0
       AND `my spouse's qualified earned income` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN 0.7% TIMES (min 50000 (min `my qualified earned income` `my spouse's qualified earned income`))
    ELSE 0

GIVEN `tax year` IS A NUMBER
      `filing status` IS A `Filing status`
      y IS A `My year`
      `federal EITC` IS A NUMBER
      `my qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER
      `my spouse's qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER
      `IRC s 21 creditable expenses` IS A NUMBER
      `published schedule` IS A MAYBE `Rate schedule`
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`the South Carolina tax of a full-year resident` MEANS
    IF `tax year` LESS THAN 2025
    THEN `not covered` `the rates for tax years 2022 to 2024 depend on the top-rate phase-down in s 12-6-510(B)(3), which is not encoded`
    ELSE IF `tax year` EQUALS 2025
         THEN `answered` (`tax after credits` `the 2025 Tax Computation Schedule`)
         ELSE CONSIDER `published schedule`
              WHEN JUST s THEN `answered` (`tax after credits` s)
              WHEN NOTHING THEN `not covered` `the rate schedule for that tax year is not in the deposited text: give the department's published figures for the year`
    WHERE
        GIVEN s IS A `Rate schedule`
        GIVETH A NUMBER
        `tax after credits` s MEANS
            max 0 (`tax by the schedule` s (`South Carolina taxable income` y)
                   MINUS (    (125% TIMES `federal EITC`)
                         PLUS `the two wage earner credit` `filing status` `my qualified earned income` `my spouse's qualified earned income`
                         PLUS (7% TIMES `IRC s 21 creditable expenses`)))

§§ `Questions for the taxpayer`

@export What is my South Carolina taxable income for the year, as a full-year resident (S.C. Code ss 12-6-1110 to 12-6-1170)?
GIVEN `my residence` IS A `Residence` @desc Were you domiciled in South Carolina for the whole year ("resident for the whole year"), for part of it ("part-year resident"), or not at all ("nonresident")?
      `my federal taxable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your federal taxable income for the year in US dollars (Form 1040), joint with your spouse if you file jointly
      `interest on other states' bonds` IS A NUMBER @desc Interest on bonds of states other than South Carolina, or of their cities and counties, that was tax-free federally
      `interest on US obligations` IS A NUMBER @desc Interest on US Treasury and other federal obligations that is in your federal taxable income
      `state income tax refund` IS A NUMBER @desc Any state income tax refund included in your federal income
      `taxable social security` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of your Social Security and Tier 1 Railroad Retirement benefits that is taxable federally
      `I itemized` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you itemized deductions on your federal return
      `state and local income taxes I deducted` IS A NUMBER @desc State and local income taxes included in your federal itemized deductions (0 if you did not itemize)
      `my itemized deductions` IS A NUMBER @desc Your total federal itemized deductions (0 if you did not itemize)
      `the standard deduction I could have taken` IS A NUMBER @desc The federal standard deduction you would have had if you had not itemized
      `my net capital gain` IS A NUMBER @desc Your net capital gain for the year as defined in IRC s 1222
      `my dependents` IS A NUMBER @desc How many dependents (qualifying children and qualifying relatives) you can claim
      `my dependents under six` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those dependents had not reached age 6 during the year
      `the dependent exemption for the year` IS A NUMBER @desc The South Carolina dependent exemption for the year as adjusted by the Department of Revenue ($4,110 before indexing)
      `I reach 65 this year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are 65 or will turn 65 during the year
      `my retirement income` IS A NUMBER @desc Retirement income you received as the original owner of a 401, 403(b), IRA, 457 or public employee or military retirement plan, included in your income and not subject to an early-withdrawal penalty
      `my spouse is on my return` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you file a joint return with your spouse
      `my spouse reaches 65 this year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your spouse is 65 or turns 65 during the year (ignored if not joint)
      `my spouse's retirement income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your spouse's retirement income as described above (ignored if not joint)
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`What is my South Carolina taxable income` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my residence`
    WHEN `resident for the whole year` THEN `answered` (`South Carolina taxable income` (`My year` WITH
        `federal taxable income`                                       IS `my federal taxable income`
        `interest on other states' obligations excluded federally`     IS `interest on other states' bonds`
        `interest on US obligations included federally`                IS `interest on US obligations`
        `state income tax refund included federally`                   IS `state income tax refund`
        `social security and tier 1 railroad benefits taxed federally` IS `taxable social security`
        `I itemized federally`                                         IS `I itemized`
        `state and local income taxes in my federal itemized deductions` IS `state and local income taxes I deducted`
        `my federal itemized deductions`                               IS `my itemized deductions`
        `the standard deduction I would have had`                      IS `the standard deduction I could have taken`
        `net capital gain`                                             IS `my net capital gain`
        `dependents`                                                   IS `my dependents`
        `dependents under six`                                         IS `my dependents under six`
        `the dependent exemption amount for the year`                  IS `the dependent exemption for the year`
        `I reach 65 this year`                                         IS `I reach 65 this year`
        `my retirement income`                                         IS `my retirement income`
        `my spouse is on this return`                                  IS `my spouse is on my return`
        `my spouse reaches 65 this year`                               IS `my spouse reaches 65 this year`
        `my spouse's retirement income`                                IS `my spouse's retirement income`))
    OTHERWISE `not covered` `a nonresident or part-year resident computes South Carolina taxable income under Article 13 (ss 12-6-1710, 12-6-1720), which is not encoded`

@export What is the South Carolina income tax on a given South Carolina taxable income for tax year 2025, before credits (S.C. Code s 12-6-510 and the 2025 Tax Computation Schedule)?
GIVEN `my South Carolina taxable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your South Carolina taxable income for 2025 in US dollars (SC1040 line 5)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is my 2025 South Carolina tax before credits` MEANS
    `tax by the schedule` `the 2025 Tax Computation Schedule` `my South Carolina taxable income`

@export How much is my South Carolina earned income tax credit (S.C. Code s 12-6-3632)?
GIVEN `my residence` IS A `Residence` @desc Were you domiciled in South Carolina for the whole year ("resident for the whole year"), for part of it ("part-year resident"), or not at all ("nonresident")?
      `my federal EITC` IS A NUMBER @desc The federal earned income tax credit allowed to you under IRC s 32 for the year, in US dollars
      `my South Carolina tax before credits` IS A NUMBER @desc Your South Carolina income tax under s 12-6-510 before credits
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much is my South Carolina EITC` MEANS
    IF `my residence` EQUALS `resident for the whole year`
    THEN min (125% TIMES `my federal EITC`) (max 0 `my South Carolina tax before credits`)
    ELSE 0

@export How much is our South Carolina two wage earner credit, as a married couple filing jointly (S.C. Code s 12-6-3330)?
GIVEN `my filing status` IS A `Filing status` @desc Your federal filing status: single, married filing jointly, married filing separately, head of household, or surviving spouse
      `my qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your South Carolina qualified earned income: wages and self-employment earnings taxable in South Carolina less the IRC s 62(a)(1), (2), (6), (7) and (12) deductions allocable to them
      `my spouse's qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your spouse's South Carolina qualified earned income, worked out the same way
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much is our two wage earner credit` MEANS
    `the two wage earner credit` `my filing status` `my qualified earned income` `my spouse's qualified earned income`

@export How much South Carolina income tax do I owe for the year, as a full-year resident, after the earned income, two wage earner and child care credits?
GIVEN `the tax year` IS A NUMBER @desc The tax year, for example 2025
      `my filing status` IS A `Filing status` @desc Your federal filing status: single, married filing jointly, married filing separately, head of household, or surviving spouse
      `my federal taxable income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your federal taxable income for the year in US dollars (Form 1040), joint with your spouse if you file jointly
      `interest on other states' bonds` IS A NUMBER @desc Interest on bonds of states other than South Carolina, or of their cities and counties, that was tax-free federally
      `interest on US obligations` IS A NUMBER @desc Interest on US Treasury and other federal obligations that is in your federal taxable income
      `state income tax refund` IS A NUMBER @desc Any state income tax refund included in your federal income
      `taxable social security` IS A NUMBER @desc The part of your Social Security and Tier 1 Railroad Retirement benefits that is taxable federally
      `I itemized` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you itemized deductions on your federal return
      `state and local income taxes I deducted` IS A NUMBER @desc State and local income taxes included in your federal itemized deductions (0 if you did not itemize)
      `my itemized deductions` IS A NUMBER @desc Your total federal itemized deductions (0 if you did not itemize)
      `the standard deduction I could have taken` IS A NUMBER @desc The federal standard deduction you would have had if you had not itemized
      `my net capital gain` IS A NUMBER @desc Your net capital gain for the year as defined in IRC s 1222
      `my dependents` IS A NUMBER @desc How many dependents (qualifying children and qualifying relatives) you can claim
      `my dependents under six` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those dependents had not reached age 6 during the year
      `the dependent exemption for the year` IS A NUMBER @desc The South Carolina dependent exemption for the year as adjusted by the Department of Revenue ($4,110 before indexing)
      `I reach 65 this year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are 65 or will turn 65 during the year
      `my retirement income` IS A NUMBER @desc Retirement income you received as the original owner of a 401, 403(b), IRA, 457 or public employee or military retirement plan, included in your income and not subject to an early-withdrawal penalty
      `my spouse reaches 65 this year` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your spouse is 65 or turns 65 during the year (ignored unless married filing jointly)
      `my spouse's retirement income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your spouse's retirement income as described above (ignored unless married filing jointly)
      `my federal EITC` IS A NUMBER @desc The federal earned income tax credit allowed to you under IRC s 32
      `my qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your South Carolina qualified earned income (for the two wage earner credit)
      `my spouse's qualified earned income` IS A NUMBER @desc Your spouse's South Carolina qualified earned income (0 unless married filing jointly)
      `my child care expenses that count under IRC s 21` IS A NUMBER @desc Your employment-related child and dependent care expenses that count for the federal credit under IRC s 21 after its dollar limits, and that relate to South Carolina income
GIVETH AN `Answer` NUMBER
`How much South Carolina tax do I owe` MEANS
    `the South Carolina tax of a full-year resident` `the tax year` `my filing status` (`My year` WITH
        `federal taxable income`                                       IS `my federal taxable income`
        `interest on other states' obligations excluded federally`     IS `interest on other states' bonds`
        `interest on US obligations included federally`                IS `interest on US obligations`
        `state income tax refund included federally`                   IS `state income tax refund`
        `social security and tier 1 railroad benefits taxed federally` IS `taxable social security`
        `I itemized federally`                                         IS `I itemized`
        `state and local income taxes in my federal itemized deductions` IS `state and local income taxes I deducted`
        `my federal itemized deductions`                               IS `my itemized deductions`
        `the standard deduction I would have had`                      IS `the standard deduction I could have taken`
        `net capital gain`                                             IS `my net capital gain`
        `dependents`                                                   IS `my dependents`
        `dependents under six`                                         IS `my dependents under six`
        `the dependent exemption amount for the year`                  IS `the dependent exemption for the year`
        `I reach 65 this year`                                         IS `I reach 65 this year`
        `my retirement income`                                         IS `my retirement income`
        `my spouse is on this return`                                  IS (`my filing status` EQUALS `married filing jointly`)
        `my spouse reaches 65 this year`                               IS `my spouse reaches 65 this year`
        `my spouse's retirement income`                                IS `my spouse's retirement income`)
        `my federal EITC` `my qualified earned income` `my spouse's qualified earned income` `my child care expenses that count under IRC s 21` NOTHING
